Thought: To efficiently determine the SMILES string of the organic compound, I should first gather some initial data about the compound. The mass spectrometry spectra can provide the molecular weight, which is a good starting point for deducing the molecular formula. This will help narrow down the possible structures.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The mass spectrometry data indicates a molecular ion peak at m/z 114.068, which suggests the molecular weight of the compound is approximately 114. This information can be used to deduce the molecular formula. Next, I will use the molecular weight to find possible isomers and their SMILES strings.
Action: obtain_isomers_from_molecular_formula
Action Input: {"molecular_formula": "C6H10O"}

Thought: I have obtained a list of possible isomers for the molecular formula C6H10O. To identify the correct SMILES string, I should compare these isomers against the NMR data. I will start by obtaining the proton NMR spectra to see which isomer matches the observed chemical shifts.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Since the proton NMR prediction failed, I will try using the carbon NMR spectra to differentiate between the isomers. This can provide information about the carbon environment in the compound.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Both the proton and carbon NMR predictions have failed, which limits the direct comparison of NMR data with the isomers. As an alternative, I will use the search_by_smiles tool to find the most relevant entries from the NMRShift database for each isomer. This will help identify which isomer matches the expected NMR data.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1CC(C2C1C2)O", "top_k": 1}

Thought: The search result for the SMILES "C1CC(C2C1C2)O" did not match the expected NMR data, as the returned entry is for a different compound. I will proceed to check the next isomer in the list using the same method.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC[C@@H](C)C#CO", "top_k": 1}

Thought: The search result for the SMILES "CC[C@@H](C)C#CO" also did not match the expected NMR data, as the returned entry is for a different compound. I will continue to check the next isomer in the list.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC1C[C@H]1C(=O)C", "top_k": 1}

Thought: The search result for the SMILES "CC1C[C@H]1C(=O)C" also did not match the expected NMR data, as the returned entry is for a different compound. I will proceed to check the next isomer in the list.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(=CCC[CH2+])[O-]", "top_k": 1}

Thought: The search result for the SMILES "CC(=CCC[CH2+])[O-]" also did not match the expected NMR data, as the returned entry is for a different compound. I will check the last isomer in the list.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C=C1CC1CCO", "top_k": 1}

Thought: None of the isomers matched the expected NMR data from the NMRShift database. Given the constraints and the lack of direct NMR data, I will validate the SMILES strings to ensure they are correct and then select the most plausible one based on the molecular formula and mass spectrometry data.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1CC(C2C1C2)O"}

Thought: The SMILES "C1CC(C2C1C2)O" is valid. I will proceed to validate the next SMILES string to ensure all options are correct before making a final decision.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC[C@@H](C)C#CO"}

